# Line-ending rules. Prevention, not a fix for anything currently broken - mvnw is already
# LF in both the index and the working tree (verify with `git ls-files --eol`).
#
# The failure being guarded against: a shell script authored on Windows and committed with
# CRLF is unexecutable on Linux. The kernel reads the shebang as "/bin/sh\r", an interpreter
# that does not exist, and reports
#     bad interpreter: No such file or directory
# The file looks correct in every editor. It only fails on the machine that matters, which
# is exactly the class of problem CI exists to catch.
#
# Scoped to the files where endings are load-bearing rather than a blanket "* text=auto",
# which would renormalise the whole repository and bury real changes in a huge diff.

# Executed by /bin/sh on the CI runner - must be LF
mvnw        text eol=lf
*.sh        text eol=lf

# Executed by cmd.exe - must be CRLF
mvnw.cmd    text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf
